# {{project_name}}

{{project_description}}

A C++20 Hello World application targeting 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie.

## Develop from an Apple Silicon Mac

Install `just` and a Docker CLI connected to an ARM64 Linux daemon. Docker Desktop
is not required; Colima is supported. Keep this project in a directory shared
with the daemon (normally your home directory).

```sh
just container-init
just container-ci
just container-run
just container-artifact
```

`container-build` builds the development image; `container-shell` opens an
interactive shell. All commands force `linux/arm64`. The image uses a pinned
Debian base and dated apt archive, with GCC 14, LLVM 19, GoogleTest, a hash-locked Semgrep environment, and the
validation tools. Network access is needed while building the image; application
builds do not fetch dependencies. `container-artifact` builds a separate runtime
image containing the installed executable and its runtime libraries, then checks
its output. The runtime runs without root privileges.

## Develop on the Pi

The same development container runs on the Pi. Alternatively install the tools
listed in the Dockerfile natively, including its pinned Semgrep environment, and
put LLVM 19 binaries on PATH. `just check` fails if tools or the supported compiler
majors are missing. Then run `just init`, `just ci`, and `just run`.

The executable prints exactly `Hello, world!` followed by a newline. Extra
arguments are usage errors. An output failure is a runtime error. Exit codes are
0 for success, 1 for runtime failure, and 2 for usage error. Run the executable
directly when stdout must contain only the result; `just` adds workflow status.

## Structure

- `src/cli/`: command-line boundary and process exit status.
- `src/application/`: application operation and explicit output dependency.
- `src/domain/`: pure greeting value.
- `include/`: self-contained declaration-only public headers; implementations live in compiled sources.
- `tests/`: GoogleTest unit tests and header compilation checks.
- `scripts/`: validation and CLI/installed-artifact tests.
- `config/`: local policy rules and spelling vocabulary.

## Validation

`just ci` checks prerequisites first and fails on the first failed gate:
formatting, clang-tidy, cppcheck, Flawfinder, local Semgrep policies, secrets,
shell scripts, spelling, architecture/source inventory, GCC/Clang debug and
release tests, sanitizers, coverage, ELF hardening, and installed artifacts.
`ci-quiet` runs the identical sequence with concise output. CI never reformats
source. Use `code-format` explicitly to format it.

Coverage enforces {{coverage_threshold}}% separately for project lines, functions,
and branches; zero branch opportunities are reported honestly. Empty tests,
skipped tests, missing reports, missing source coverage, and orphan source files
fail. Coverage runs use LLVM source instrumentation, and HTML is written under
`reports/coverage-html/`. `code-memcheck` runs Valgrind separately. `debug` opens
GDB; `strace` and LLVM disassembly tools are available in the image.

The compiler target is explicitly `armv8-a`; never use `-march=native` for Pi
artifacts built on a Mac. CMake probes `<arm_neon.h>` support. The starter has no
SIMD kernel: add intrinsics only to a dedicated implementation with scalar
reference tests when measured application work warrants them. Define overflow,
vector-tail, alignment, and floating-point behavior first. Do not enable global
fast-math. Add bounded libFuzzer tests once there is a parser or data boundary;
do not claim fuzz or mutation coverage for this constant-output application.

A Linux container does not validate Raspberry Pi peripherals, kernel-specific
behavior, or performance. Test deployment on the actual Pi, and benchmark only
on a named stable Pi runner. The container base and target OS must have compatible
runtime libraries; ARM64 alone is not a deployment guarantee.
